Q: HTML AutoPlay next episode similar to Netflix etc A: <p>well, you can read the length and the offset of the video consecutively to dteermine when to preload. the event to watch for is called &quot;timeupdate&quot;. if you wanted to track the end event that would be &quot;ended&quot;.</p>
<p>this would print the current time youre in the video and the duration.</p>
<pre><code>document.getElementById(&quot;#videoElement&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;timeupdate&quot;, (event)=&gt;{

console.log(event.target.currentTime);

console.log(event.target.duration);

})
</code></pre>
<p><code>(duration-currentTime)/1000 &gt;== 30</code> would be the check for whether only 30 seconds or less are left in paly time. maybe thats no good for videos only lasting 30 seconds or less in total.</p>

Q: Receive a string and verify if it is a valid date with date-fns A: <p>I'm facing a similar problem and I think this also solves it.
I prefer this solution as the parsing is a bit more explicit than the locale object.</p>
<pre><code>import { parse, isValid, format } from &quot;date-fns&quot;;

const getFormatedDate = (date) =&gt; {
  const parsed = parse(date, &quot;dd/MM/yyyy&quot;, new Date());
  if (isValid(parsed)) {
    return format(parsed, &quot;dd-MM-yyyy&quot;);
  }
  return null;
};

console.log(getFormatedDate(&quot;31/04/1989&quot;)); // invalid: april has only 30 days
console.log(getFormatedDate(&quot;30/04/1989&quot;));
</code></pre>
